            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use the required prime factors and the divisor-count formula to obtain ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "2^4\\times3^2\\times5\\times7",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ".",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Since $8,9,10$ and $7$ divide the number, its prime factorisation contains at least $2^3\\times3^2\\times5\\times7$. These factors also ensure divisibility by $6$.\n\nWrite $n=2^a3^b5^c7^d$, initially allowing additional prime factors, where $a\\ge3$, $b\\ge2$ and $c,d\\ge1$. The required primes already contribute at least $4\\times3\\times2\\times2=48$ divisors. An additional prime would give at least $96$ divisors, so there are no other primes. If either $c$ or $d$ exceeded $1$, there would be at least $4\\times3\\times3\\times2=72$ divisors. Thus $c=d=1$.\n\nThe divisor-count formula gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(a+1)(b+1)\\times2\\times2&=60\\\\(a+1)(b+1)&=15.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $a+1\\ge4$ and $b+1\\ge3$, the only possible factor pair is $a+1=5$, $b+1=3$. Hence $a=4$ and $b=2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}n&=2^4\\times3^2\\times5\\times7\\\\n&=5040.\\end{align*}$$\nCheck: $5040$ is divisible by every listed number and has $(4+1)(2+1)(1+1)(1+1)=60$ factors.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
